    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s44407-026-00064-7",
    "abstract": "Abstract Geopolitical tensions in Eastern Europe underscore the urgency of addressing the climatic and radiological consequences of a regional nuclear conflict. Using an Earth System Model, we simulate a hypothetical nuclear conflict at the Ukraine-Russia border that releases 5 Teragram (Tg; 5 million tons) of black carbon (BC), the dominant driver of the post-detonation climate response, into the stratosphere. We adopt the 5 Tg BC post-detonation emission scenario because it is a widely modelled scenario for a \u201climited\u201d regional nuclear conflict (typically India\u2013Pakistan), enabling a direct comparison with prior studies. The extended stratospheric lifetime of BC induces hemispheric climate disruption: the Northern Hemisphere cools by ~1 \u00b0C in year-1, with anomalies of \u22125 \u00b0C in Russia and \u22124 \u00b0C in the United States; surface solar radiation declines by ~30 W m \u22122 over the US; and precipitation decreases by ~40% across mid-latitude croplands. Stratospheric warming alters subtropical and polar jets, displacing the Intertropical Convergence Zone ~2\u20136\u00b0 southward, delaying climate recovery by ~6 years. Long-lived radionuclides transported with BC disperse globally, with ~40% depositing in the Southern Hemisphere. These findings underscore the importance of nuclear-risk reduction and provide a robust benchmark for food-security and humanitarian-impact assessments.",
    "field": "Environmental consequences of nuclear conflict",
    "problem": "Previous studies of nuclear conflict aftermath have focused on superpower or South Asian scenarios, lacking a physically consistent assessment of the coupled climate disruption and radiological fallout from a potential conflict in Eastern Europe, a region of current geopolitical tension.",
    "method": "Using an Earth System Model, the study simulates a hypothetical nuclear conflict at the Ukraine-Russia border by releasing 5 Tg of black carbon into the stratosphere to assess the subsequent global climate disruption and dispersal of radioactive fallout.",
    "task": "The task is to simulate the global climatic disruption and radiological fallout from a hypothetical nuclear conflict in Eastern Europe using an Earth System Model.",
    "model": [
        "UKESM1.1"
    ],
